1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:03,933
Every LLM app reads text it didn't write.

2
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:08,733
This is Breakwater: a firewall for LLM apps that attacks itself to prove it works.

3
00:00:09,067 --> 00:00:13,333
Here's the problem: a model can't tell your instructions from the text it reads.

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:18,333
One hidden line in a document, and the assistant obeys the attacker instead of you.

5
00:00:18,533 --> 00:00:22,200
Attacks on LLM apps come in four shapes.

6
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:25,933
Direct injection: a user simply tells the model to ignore its rules.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,600
Indirect injection: instructions hidden in PDFs, web pages or tool results.

8
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:33,267
Data leakage: secrets and personal data slipping out in prompts or replies.

9
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:36,933
Tool abuse: tricking an agent into calling its tools for the attacker.

10
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:42,067
Against an unprotected app, 98% of our 75 test attacks succeed.

11
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,533
Breakwater sits between your app and the model. Every request and reply passes through it.

12
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:53,133
It checks text in layers, cheapest first: normalising, fast rules, an optional classifier, then an LLM judge.

13
00:00:53,333 --> 00:01:00,400
A normal question goes straight through. If the reply leaks a key, it is redacted on the way back.

14
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:04,733
An obvious injection is stopped by the rules, in about 20 milliseconds.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,733
Borderline text goes to the LLM judge, which makes the call. Here it blocks with 0.99 confidence.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:19,400
The same checks protect documents before they are indexed and agent tool calls before they run.

17
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:25,067
We built a red team into the project: 75 attacks, two variants each, and 100 harmless prompts.

18
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:32,333
Rules alone cut attack success from 98% to 44%. With the classifier it falls to 1.3%, at 7% false positives.

19
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:39,800
In CI, the same harness fails the build if a change lets more attacks through.

20
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:47,200
To use it, run Breakwater next to your app: one command starts the gateway, database and dashboard.

21
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:55,200
Then point your app at it. Any OpenAI-compatible client works: change the base URL and the key.

22
00:01:55,467 --> 00:02:03,200
For RAG and agents, the Python SDK scans documents, marks retrieved text as untrusted, and guards tool calls.

23
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:11,133
And run the red team against your own app in CI, so a weaker policy can never ship unnoticed.

24
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:16,400
Breakwater was built spec first, in five milestones, each one tested before the next began.

25
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:23,400
Proxy and auth, detectors, document and tool protection, the red team, then the classifier, judge and dashboard.

26
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:30,667
886 automated tests and 66 Docker smoke checks, and every number in this video comes from the harness.

27
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:36,533
Python, FastAPI, Postgres, RE2, ONNX Runtime and React, built with Claude Code.

28
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:44,867
Breakwater: the LLM firewall that attacks itself. Find it on GitHub.
